# remote -- caps.decl
#
# Wave: SS (Wave SS 5-issue cohort)  Milestone: M1-001 (repo bootstrap)
#
# This repo ships two binaries out of one source tree:
#   `remote` (src/remote_client.pdx, Module RemoteClient) -- authenticate
#            + spawn a remote shell + tty bridge, over a TCP connection
#            to <host>:22.
#   `rcopy`  (src/rcopy.pdx, Module Rcopy) -- authenticate + stream a
#            local file to <host>:<remote-path> over the same
#            KEM+AEAD-wrapped channel (op-dispatch on one daemon port,
#            no separate SCP-style port).
#
# Both share src/kem.pdx (Module Kem, ML-KEM-768 handshake) and
# src/channel.pdx (Module Channel, ChaCha20-Poly1305 framed transport).
# Neither shared module issues a syscall or cap-invoke of its own that
# is not already covered by a row below -- both are called from within
# the entry binaries' own stack frames, so the capability surface is
# the union of what `remote` and `rcopy` each need, not a superset per
# module.
#
# Format: `requires:` lists cap-kind names + narrowed rights + optional
# scope, mirroring the discipline documented at tools/user/cp/caps.decl
# (paideia-os repo) -- the caps-decl reader resolves each name to its
# KIND_* ordinal at exec time and fails-fast on a mismatch between the
# loader-seeded cap set and this list.
requires:
  # Identity root. KIND_USER = 0x190 (paideia-os R48.M1 substrate,
  # src/kernel/core/cap/kind_user.pdx). Present on both binaries so the
  # invoking user's identity is available to a future audit-journal
  # correlation of remote-session start/end.
  - KIND_USER (self)

  # Network egress. The kernel's real cap-adjacent gate for the socket
  # family is KIND_TCP_SOCKET (0x1AB, src/kernel/core/cap/kind.pdx
  # R72.M1-006) -- there is no separate KIND_TCP_CONNECT ordinal in the
  # kernel's kind table. This row declares KIND_TCP_SOCKET narrowed to
  # the `connect` right against the caller-supplied <host> argument,
  # which is the capability this repo's own design vocabulary calls
  # "KIND_TCP_CONNECT" (M1-001 issue text). The two names describe the
  # same authority; this file uses the real kernel ordinal so a future
  # manifest reconciler does not fail on an unknown kind, and records
  # the M1-001 vocabulary mapping here for provenance.
  - KIND_TCP_SOCKET (connect, <host>)

  # Interactive tty control. KIND_TTY = 0x197 (paideia-os R30-PREP
  # substrate, src/kernel/core/cap/kind_tty.pdx). `remote` uses this to
  # flip the local tty into raw mode (TTY_OP_SET_RAW / TTY_OP_SET_COOKED,
  # ordinals 7/8) for the interactive shell bridge; `rcopy` declares the
  # same row for symmetry with the shared Channel module's diagnostic
  # writes but does not invoke SET_RAW.
  #
  # NOTE (known kernel-side gap, documented at tools/user/shell/src/
  # line_reader.pdx lr_tty_set_raw): KIND_TTY is not yet in
  # KIND_SEEDABLE_TABLE and no ring-3 process is seeded a KIND_TTY row
  # at boot today (paideia-os design/architecture.md §3.3 items 3a/3b).
  # This declaration is real and the call sites in src/remote_client.pdx
  # are real cap_invoke calls -- but until a loader-side KIND_TTY seed
  # lands for non-shell processes, `rc_tty_set_raw` fails closed
  # (returns an error, the pump loop still runs over cooked-mode I/O)
  # rather than silently no-op-ing. Same posture as cp's KIND_PDXFS_VOL
  # declare-before-invoke row.
  - KIND_TTY (invoke)

  # Local filesystem access for `rcopy`'s source file. Narrowed to READ
  # against the caller-supplied local path; the loader narrows the cap
  # tail's subtree to the argument-path prefix at exec, matching cp's
  # own KIND_PDXFS_FILE(read, <src>) discipline.
  - KIND_PDXFS_FILE (read, <local-src>)
